    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "html": "<p>Carthage was once the capital city of the ancient Carthaginian civilization.&nbsp;For much of its history, the city was on hostile terms with the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magna_Graecia\">Greeks in Sicily</a>&nbsp;and with the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Republic\">Roman Republic</a>, leading to a series of armed conflicts known as the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sicilian_Wars\">Greek-Punic Wars</a>&nbsp;(c. 600–265 BC) and the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punic_Wars\">Punic Wars</a>(264–146 BC) respectively (source: Wikipedia). It's pretty amazing to walk around while keeping this history in mind.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "gallery",
      "columns": 1,
      "images": [
        {
          "filename": "26A_3.jpg",
          "src_url": "https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/58d6f67f9de4bba5f5e41958/1490638883608-9KOZDSB4UXGT60Z0G1KE/26A_3.jpg?format=original"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "html": "<p>We had a chance to explore the <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baths_of_Antoninus\">Ancient Roman Baths</a> in Carthage, which is a UNESCO World Heritage Site (!!!). Considering I'm a big fan of baths, I was pretty excited. We also checked out the <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g293754-d480611-Reviews-Carthage_Museum-Carthage_Tunis_Governorate.html\">Carthage Museum</a>&nbsp;and the <a target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://www.tripadvisor.com/Attraction_Review-g293754-d1860848-Reviews-The_Roman_theatre_of_Carthage-Carthage_Tunis_Governorate.html\">Roman Theatre of Carthage</a>&nbsp;as well.</p>"
    },
